hard.problem:
Let $P(x), Q(x), T(x)$ be polynomials with nonnegative integer coefficients. Suppose that
\begin{align*}
T(x) &= (x^2 - 3x + 3) P(x) \\
T(x) &= \left(\frac{x^2}{20} - \frac{x}{15} + \frac{1}{12}\right) Q(x).
\end{align*}
Additionally, assume that $P(x), Q(x), T(x)$ are all monic, that $\deg T = 6$, and that every coefficient of $T(x)$ is at most $10$. Does such a triple $(P, Q, T)$ exist?

hard.reference_solution:
No. The second identity is 60T=(3x^2-4x+5)Q. Since T and Q are monic, the leading coefficients on the two sides would be 60 and 3 respectively, an impossibility. Equivalently Q would need leading coefficient 20, contrary to monicity. The degree bound and coefficient cap do not resolve this contradiction.

Final answer:
\boxed{No.}

